// Pull request author: 0xtamseel
// Change: new rule
// Title: QoS ThrottleRate Set by WMI
// Sigma ID: e4b2d9a1-8f3a-4c9b-9e2a-1d7f6b5a3c21
// Status: experimental | Level: medium
// Author: Tamseel Ahmad
// Date: 2026-10-13 | Modified: -
// ATT&CK: defense-impairment, T1687, T1685
// Description: Detects when a Quality of Service (QoS) Throttle Rate policy is created/updated by the Windows
//   Management Instrumentation (WMI) provider service (wmiprvse.exe). Threat actors use WMI to
//   remotely script or automate changes. They abuse this feature to choke outbound EDR connection,
//   ultimately bypassing EDR, slow down data leaks to bypass filters, or cause intentional denial-
//   of-service conditions on critical communication apps.
// False positives:
//   - Legitimate QOS throttle modification
// References:
//   - https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/networking/technologies/qos/qos-policy-top
//   - https://www.zerosalarium.com/2026/06/edrchoker-choking-telemetry-stream-block-edr.html
//   - https://github.com/TwoSevenOneT/EDRChoker/
//   - https://www.ipurple.team/2026/06/17/qos-policies/
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/ffa52761739f35715ac8b21bba0b6a4d9844879a/rules/windows/registry/registry_event/registry_event_wmi_qos_throttlerate.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language, Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.
// Note: 1 wildcard comparison(s) rewritten to 'matches regex' (KQL treats * in contains/endswith/== as a literal character)

DeviceRegistryEvents
| where InitiatingProcessFolderPath endswith "\\wmiprvse.exe" and (RegistryKey matches regex @"(?i)\\Software\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\QoS.*$" and RegistryKey contains "ThrottleRate")
